# The three bank-patch tables are GENERATED but TRACKED, and unlike
# kernel_exports.s they cannot simply be ignored: each is an INPUT to the very
# assembly whose listing generates it, so there is no clean-checkout state in
# which the build could produce them from nothing.
#
# `-merge` stops git from attempting a line-by-line merge of two address lists.
# That merge is never meaningful -- both sides are stale the moment the kernel's
# size changes, and neither is correct for the merged result -- but it SUCCEEDS
# often enough to be dangerous, silently producing a table that matches no
# build.  With this, git leaves the conflict for a human, which for these files
# means one thing: take either side, rebuild, and regenerate.
#
# THE REBUILD IS WHAT MAKES THIS SAFE, and it is not a matter of remembering.
# All three are --check'd by the `kchecks` ninja rule, so a table that does not
# match the listing FAILS THE BUILD; and the two bank-patch tables carry
# SYMBOLIC entries (`DA _kfp342+3`), which the assembler refuses outright when
# the site named has been compiled out.  A stale table cannot reach a booting
# image (apple/fgx#79).
#
#     python3 tools/gen_far_bankpatch.py build/fgx_S01_KernelMain_Output.txt \
#         src/kernel/far_patchsites.s core
#     python3 tools/gen_w5100_patchsites.py build/fgx_S01_KernelMain_Output.txt \
#         src/kernel/w5100_patchsites.s
#     python3 tools/gen_far_bankpatch.py build/fgxfar_Output.txt \
#         src/kernel/far/core_patchsites.s far
#
# If the first build after a merge fails with "Impossible to evaluate DA Data",
# that is this working: delete the offending DA line, build, regenerate, build.
src/kernel/far_patchsites.s        -merge
src/kernel/far/core_patchsites.s   -merge
src/kernel/w5100_patchsites.s      -merge
